When I was applying to UT, the most annoying thing was the lack of a verifiable reference point, someone successful I could compare myself to and gauge whether I was on track for admission. I'm writing this to give prospective appliers what I didn't have.

This is not a guide on what you need to (or should) do to get in. If you're looking for the step-by-step requirements, I wrote about that in another [blog post](https://transfer4engr.com/blog/transferring-into-ut-ece).

Before we start, I want to mention that my way of viewing transfer admissions is that you are no longer trying to get the admissions committee to know you so well they can't bear to reject you, you are proving your ability to succeed in one environment that is comparable to another, potentially more prestigious environment. Each leadership role is an interview you got through, networking completed, etc. - things that show you can succeed on one campus show you can succeed on another.

## My Profile in 15 Seconds

4.00 college GPA, up to calc 2 and physics 1, officer on 2 clubs, member of 2 competition robotics teams (no results from competitions), UROP grant and research related to that, strong LOR from research advisor, around a 9/10 essay with specific reasons to transfer.

## Where I Started

I went to the University of Oklahoma, and I went in with the intention of keeping the door of transferring open - I wanted to give OU a chance, then go from there.

