Notice that this schedule is not particularly *hard.* There are no honors classes, no differential equations or E&M physics. Your goal should not be to do well in hard classes, it should be perfection (or close to it) in run-of-the-mill classes. A 4.00 will look better on an application than any hard class will.

## Extracurriculars

I thought of my EC's as either "college application" activities or "resume" activities. Every resume activity is a good college application activity, but not every activity that looks good on a college application looks good on a resume. I tried to balance these, with the officer positions falling onto the side of looking great on my college application and my competition stuff looking good on a resume. I feel doing this gives your profile a more rounded feel, not purely technical while showcasing ability to get involved on campus.

In order of what I think their importance was:

IEEE robotics - This was the first year for this team, so I got a role as a sub team (vision) lead. I was aware of this opening because I did my research before the semester started, and had applied during the short window applications were open.

IEEE Event Committee Officer - Same story for getting this role, research before the semester led me to find openings, openings which could only be applied for before the semester started, leading me to have little competition despite knowing nobody at the time.

Engineers Serving Others Freshman Executive - I found out about this by going to those large club outreach meetings and being personable in the interview. For these roles, it's not expected to be technically strong, you just have to be friendly and make an impression. Having a role like this on your college resume tells admissions committees that someone else has done the grunt work and screened you for personality, and is a positive sign of being a good fit on another campus.

Combots (combat robots) Competition Member - I joined this one with a few friends, and we never really ended up getting anywhere because of a lack of consistency with going to meetings. I recommend having fun with friends, but not relying on them to get work done.

## Research

Almost without a doubt, research was the most important thing on my resume (both for college and for internships). It is the most rigorous to get into, and therefore the strongest signal to an admissions committee that you mean business.

My experience with getting into research spanned my entire freshman year - to start, I went to networking events and outreach panels, with zero success despite technically doing everything correctly. I realized something needed to change, and over winter break I looked into what different professors at OU were doing, found 10 projects/professors I was interested in, emailed all of them, got 2 responses, and ended up with 1 meeting from someone willing to give me a chance. From there, I worked with him to find something for me to work on: We settled on me applying for a UROP (Undergraduate Research Opportunity Program) grant, got it, and the rest of my spring semester consisted of 20+ hour work weeks in a field I had never actually worked in. Research is not easy - that is why is it the best thing you can have on your resume.

I got lucky with a lot of this process, however it is important to understand my mindset here: If I hadn't got responses from any of the initial 10 emails, I would've sent 20 more. If my UROP grant was denied, I would've worked on a pre-existing project; with something as important as research, you cannot roll over and give up on it at the first rejection.

